================================================================================
4. Our own code
================================================================================

Everything not listed above is original work in this repository, under the MIT
license in LICENSE. Two pieces deserve a precise statement, because "derived
from SQLite" would be the wrong description of either:

  build/shim.c -- the row-batching shim. Original C written for this
    repository. It contains no SQLite source code. It declares the prototypes
    of the eight public sqlite3_* entry points it calls (sqlite3_step,
    sqlite3_column_*) and calls them in the order any caller would, in order to
    return a whole result row across one foreign-function boundary instead of
    seven. It is compiled into the same shared library as the amalgamation
    purely so that it can make those calls without a boundary crossing of its
    own. It is a consumer of SQLite's public API, not a derivative of SQLite's
    implementation.

  build/extensions.c -- auto-registration for compiled-in extensions. Original
    C. Declares sqlite3_auto_extension and each enabled extension's init
    function, and calls them. Same relationship to SQLite: a caller of the
    published interface.

The Go binding (library.go and the rest of the root package) is original work.
It carries numeric constants and result-code names that mirror sqlite3.h --
SQLITE_OPEN_*, SQLITE_ROW, the extended result codes, and so on. Those are
interface facts, and they come from a public-domain header in any case.

The corpus under conformance/ and the benchmark harness under bench/ are
original work. bench/ is a separate Go module and pulls in third-party SQLite
implementations (modernc.org/sqlite, github.com/ncruces/go-sqlite3) purely as
measurement subjects; see bench/go.mod for their versions. Nothing in bench/ is
distributed as part of this package, and the root module does not depend on it.
